{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBSwT3EpzNw83qunBjz9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted **index 13 (Sb 0.64 / Zn 0.36)** as the best composition. It had the highest EQE I measured, 0.0107%, but I can't tell how close that is to the best in the library.\n\n**Results (all 3 EQE measurements used):**\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 13 | 0.64 | 0.0107 |\n| 20 | 0.88 | 0.0101 |\n| 5 | 0.37 | −0.0006 (effectively zero) |\n\n- **Weak signal overall:** every EQE was very low. The 2.7 eV light is probably below the bandgap of these oxides, so they absorb little of it.\n- **13 vs 20 is not a clear win:** the two differ by about 6%, which may be within measurement noise. What the data does show is that the Sb-rich half of the line clearly beats the Zn-rich half.\n- **Why I picked 13 and 20:** I ran XRD on 4 of the 5 allowed samples (indices 0, 7, 13, 20). Only 13 and 20 showed extra peaks (near Q ≈ 1.90 and 3.63 Å⁻¹) that look like the trirutile ZnSb₂O₆ structure. Index 13 sits close to that compound's composition (Sb ≈ 0.67). I'm inferring the phase from a few peaks, not a full fit.\n- **Unmeasured:** with only 3 EQE measurements, most of the line was never tested, including indices 14–19 between my two best points. The true best composition could be in any of those gaps."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":372,"cache_read_input_tokens":8350,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":372},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80b40428-b0bf-40c5-ad94-08f931e51028","uuid":"8aebe042-ebae-4645-a4b3-2da183db2c0f","timestamp":"2026-09-18T19:21:38.790Z","request_id":"req_011CfBSwSXEXPMiGe6RwK7a8"}
